+# Secure Aggregation Design & Threat Model (Aegis)
+
+Overview:
+- Replace educational masking prototype with a vetted secure-aggregation solution. Recommended options:
+  - TensorFlow Federated with secure aggregation extensions
+  - OpenMined / PySyft secure aggregation primitives
+  - Meta's SecAgg implementation or other audited libraries
+
+Threat model (high-level):
+- Adversary types: passive eavesdropper, curious server, malicious client, Byzantine clients.
+- Goals: protect individual client updates (confidentiality), ensure integrity of aggregated result, and resist poisoning attacks.
+
+Recommendations:
+- Use threshold secure aggregation to hide individual updates from server.
+- Enforce client authentication (Vault-issued JWT or OIDC) and enrollment.
+- Use attestations (device attestation) to ensure client integrity when possible.
+- Add robust poisoning detection and contribution clipping; apply DP (vetted libraries) at server-side post-aggregation.
+- Perform formal threat modelling and engage third-party security review before production.
+
+Migration steps:
+1. Select library and prototype on staging with small client clusters.
+2. Integrate enrollment & auth (Vault or OIDC).
+3. Implement audit logging (MCP) for each round and use Rekor for attestation of client lists and aggregates.
+4. Run adversarial tests: simulate Byzantine clients, replay attacks, and large-scale noise.
+5. Conduct third-party security review and pen testing.
+
